# frozen_string_literal: true

# Generated by shipshape. This file is yours now.
#
# **A command is exactly one write, in exactly one transaction.** The transaction is opened
# here rather than left to each subclass, so the law is true by construction instead of by
# convention.
#
# It answers with a Result, and the class method enforces that — a subclass that answers
# with anything else stops the run rather than teaching its callers a second shape.
#
# **A command never calls another command.** Sequencing writes is a workflow's job, and a
# command that calls one has either nested this transaction or silently widened it, with
# nobody deciding which. A workflow crossing several transactions is obliged to make each
# step idempotent and each intermediate state legal; a command that quietly became one has
# accepted none of that.
class Command
  include TypedArguments

<%- if auth -%>
  extend Permission
<%- end -%>

  # The check runs here, once, for every command — not in each `call`, where it would be a
  # line every operation has to remember and one operation would forget. It runs BEFORE the
  # transaction opens, so a refusal costs no lock.
  #
  # An operation implementing `anonymous_call` instead of `call` runs before anyone is
  # identified and is not checked. That is a property of the class, never of the caller.
<%- if auth -%>
  def self.call(actor: nil, **arguments)
    return Result.failure(:forbidden) unless permits?(actor)

    operation = anonymous? ? new(**arguments) : new(actor: actor, **arguments)
    result = ActiveRecord::Base.transaction { operation.__perform__ }
<%- else -%>
  def self.call(**arguments)
    result = ActiveRecord::Base.transaction { new(**arguments).__perform__ }
<%- end -%>

    raise TypeError, "#{name}#call must answer with a Result, got #{result.class}" unless result.is_a?(Result)

    result
  end

  private

  # NOTE: `return` from inside a transaction block COMMITS it. Abandon work with
  # `raise ActiveRecord::Rollback`, and answer with `failure(:code)` afterwards.
  def success(value = nil)
    Result.success(value)
  end

  def failure(error)
    Result.failure(error)
  end

  # **Reaches the private entry point with an implicit receiver**, which is the one form
  # `private` allows — `operation.call` raises, and no amount of inheritance changes that,
  # because Ruby's `private` means "no explicit receiver" rather than "inside the hierarchy".
  #
  # `send` would also work and is banned: it steps over `private`, routes around the call
  # graph, and names a method nobody can grep for. A base class that writes it cannot ask an
  # application not to.
  #
  # Public, and unreachable all the same: `new` is private, so nobody outside can hold an
  # operation to call this on.
  # `public` on purpose: this sits below the base class's own `private`, and it is called
  # with an explicit receiver from the class method above. Harmless — `new` is private, so
  # nobody outside can hold an operation to call it on.
  public def __perform__
<%- if auth -%>
    self.class.anonymous? ? anonymous_call : call
<%- else -%>
    call
<%- end -%>
  end

  # **The only way in is `call`.** A caller cannot build an operation, so it cannot reach one
  # that skipped the permission check, the transaction or the return-type assertion.
  private_class_method :new
end
